WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.310
KOBI MAGNEZI: Hello, and thank
you for joining us today.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:14.500
My name is Kobi Magnezi.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.450
I'm product manager for GKE.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.330
And today I would like to
share with you some of the best

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:23.440
practices on how to leverage
GKE to power a competitive IT

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.690
e-commerce infrastructure.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:27.880
First, let's start
with looking at some

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.400
of the drivers for
e-commerce organization

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.710
and the requirements
they imply for

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.870
the supporting infrastructure.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.600
In a recent validation study
of Google Cloud solution

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.430
for e-commerce, the ESG analysis
found that retail industry

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.430
is focused on providing
better and more differentiated

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.070
customer experience
through specifically

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.550
digital transformation
initiatives with about 50%

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.400
of retail organization
strategically prioritizing

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.900
this objective.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.260
For retailers, creating
an e-commerce presence

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.350
increases not just the
touchpoints with the customer,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.200
but also the sales
opportunity, especially when

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.020
events like the pandemic
pushes more customers

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.370
to online channels.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.680
At the same time,
it also increases

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.080
the competitive landscape.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.260
To maintain the competitiveness,
an e-commerce infrastructure

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:23.060
must provide, one, high
performance; two, scalability;

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.730
three, reliability
and availability;

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.640
four, customization
and innovation;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.470
and finally, flexibility.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.270
Let's look deeper
into these areas

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:37.630
to understand how Kubernetes
and GKE address these areas.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.170
We will also provide
some recommendation

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.870
for configuration and
options that you would want

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:44.295
to consider for your cluster.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.540
Many customers use GCP to
migrate, modernize, and improve

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.710
their deployments.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.790
GCP infrastructure and
networking are powering GKE.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.270
So when you create a
Kubernetes cluster on GKE,

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:04.370
we provision the machine and
the resources needed for GKE

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:08.810
to properly run your cluster's
control plane and nodes on GCP.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.370
The Kubernetes
cluster control plane

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:13.190
is fully managed by
us, backed by an SLA.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.410
We will keep the control
plane up to date,

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.080
and we'll scale it
automatically up and down

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.240
when cluster size changes.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.730
For efficiency and
high performance,

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.530
you can choose what
type of machine,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.990
what machine configuration
best meets your needs

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.850
for your different node pools.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.280
And as demands
change over time, so

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.520
does the load on your
e-commerce workloads.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.460
Scaling is necessary
to ensure that GKE

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.230
grows to match your need.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.200
Think about the number of
pods, the number of nodes.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.750
GKE includes several
autoscaling capabilities

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.810
that will automatically
resize your cluster based

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:53.610
on your configuration and
signals that we monitor.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.550
HPA, for example, automatically
increases or decreases

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.360
the number of pods on your
cluster based on utilization.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.170
With VPA and NPA, you
can enable vertical scale

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.770
for both your nodes and pods.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.970
And if capacity planning
is part of your practice,

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:12.700
especially for peak season
like Black Friday, Cyber Monday

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.370
or New Year, you may want
to reserve GCE capacity.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.430
With GCE reservation,
when your cluster

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.280
requires to scale,
repair, or upgrade,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.430
it will have a reserved
pool of dedicated resources

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.930
available for the performance.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:32.910
You can also create a specific
or non-specific reservation,

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:35.940
and GKE will create new
resources based on the pool

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.670
that you reserved.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.500
Now let's talk
about availability.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.360
And it's important
to distinguish

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.270
between application
availability and infrastructure

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.850
availability.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:49.260
The availability of the
business application,

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.170
which the customers
interact with,

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:55.520
and the availability of the
infrastructure, which hosts

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.890
and support the application.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.660
On the application
side, the impact

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:03.860
of insufficient availability can
be an unresponsive e-commerce

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.350
website or unreachable service.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.720
On the infrastructure
side, the impact

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.730
can happen at different levels.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.230
At the cluster
level, for example,

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:16.279
when nodes become
unresponsive or when pods get

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:19.880
rescheduled, at zone level,
or at a regional level,

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.850
where there is a connectivity
issue, for example.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.580
While both the application
and the infrastructure

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.380
impacts the overall
availability,

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.300
it is actually the
business application

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.270
that drives the decision
around redundancy, deployments,

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.170
and architecture.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.180
With this definition
in mind, it is

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:40.060
important to understand how
GKE is structured as a shared

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.070
responsibility product.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:45.550
The data plane, the nodes where
the e-commerce workloads are

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:49.720
deployed, are the most
critical piece for customers,

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:54.130
as any reliability issue can
directly impact the business.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:57.580
The Kubernetes control plane is
the next most critical piece,

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.140
as it's responsible
for scaling, repairing,

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.300
and managing the deployments.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:05.980
And then most of the workloads
deployed on the data plane

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:09.970
can probably survive an outage,
a short outage of the control

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.320
plane without too much impact.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.400
The GKE control plane
on the leftmost side

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.860
is the least critical
piece, as they are mainly

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:21.280
focused on the operations
and the management of the GKE

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.230
control plane.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:26.320
Let's look on the two
most important pieces

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.090
from a reliability perspective.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:30.950
Both control planes
and nodes can

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:34.430
be provisioned in a single
zone, a zonal cluster,

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.320
as illustrated on
the right side,

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.660
or can be provisioned
in three different zones

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.730
within a region, a
regional cluster.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:45.930
There are some cases where
zonal cluster topology better

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:48.510
serves your needs
and constraints.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:51.120
We do recommend, however,
to use regional cluster

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.820
for highly available Kubernetes
infrastructure, especially

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.760
for production workloads.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.860
For control planes,
the redundancy

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.690
provide high
availability, and in

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:04.740
turn provide higher SLA
for the Kubernetes API.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:08.850
But besides of the
API availability,

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.160
the API availability
in the control pane

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.590
doesn't really impact
the workloads themselves

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:14.610
necessarily.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:16.890
The control plane is also
responsible for maintaining

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.050
the cluster with
scaling, repairing,

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.480
and other vital operations.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.630
And you may want to take
into consideration, based

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.480
on the nature of your
e-commerce workloads and needs,

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:31.380
the type of topology that is
right for your architecture.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.150
Since we provision
compute resources

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.370
on GCP for the
Kubernetes clusters,

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:38.790
node availability are based on
the Compute Engine availability

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:42.360
for instances provisioned in
a single or multiple zone.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.060
Finally, the availability
of your application

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.000
is closely tied to
the node availability,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.050
as you can imagine.

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.300
And luckily, Kubernetes
offers an easy way

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.880
to create multiple instances
of your application

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.180
for higher availability.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.790
One great way to secure
higher availability

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:02.670
for your application, and also
to reduce potential disruption,

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:07.110
is the use of PDB, or
Pod Disruption Budget.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:11.230
PDB allows you to state
your disruption tolerance.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.130
In other words, if your
application is replicated,

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:16.140
you can specify
the minimum number

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:20.070
of replicas that can be
disrupted when maintenance even

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:20.833
happens.

00:07:20.833 --> 00:07:23.250
In the example that I have on
this slide here on the right

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:27.090
side, we specify that we cannot
tolerate less than two replicas

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.600
of our application.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.230
This is just another way of
saying max unavailable equal

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:35.130
one, in case we have three
replicas of the application.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.410
GKE respects PDB
up to 60 minutes,

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.380
and a termination grace
policy up to 60 minutes.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:44.220
When a security patch or
Kubernetes update is rolled out

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.860
on your cluster, GKE
will perform the upgrade

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.010
in a way that satisfies
the PDB configuration.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.735
The other thing
that you want GKE

00:07:52.735 --> 00:07:55.720
to know about your workloads
is what consideration

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:57.910
it needs to apply
when it schedules

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.730
your pods on different
nodes in the cluster.

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:03.070
By default, the GKE and
the Kubernetes scheduler

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:06.550
automatically spread your
pods across the nodes,

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:08.870
whether it's regional
or zonal cluster.

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:11.410
However some e-commerce
applications, and specifically

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.390
stateful workloads,
like Redis, for example,

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:17.950
require a quorum of servers
to successfully work

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:19.870
in a redundant deployment.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.390
Without specific
instruction, Kubernetes

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:24.910
may decide to
co-locate all the pods

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:27.460
for your stateful
application, stateful set

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.350
on the same machine,
on the same node,

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:33.340
and basically create a single
point of failure and risk

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:36.340
to the availability of the
e-commerce application.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:39.370
Exactly for those reasons,
we can use pod anti/affinity

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:43.539
to ensure pods are not scheduled
on the same specific node

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:45.110
if we set the criteria.

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:46.930
So if I require a
quorum of servers

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:50.050
to properly operate a stateful
application, for example,

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:52.120
I would want
Kubernetes to refrain

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:55.100
from scheduling all the
replicas on the same machine.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.340
So if something happened
to that machine,

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:00.490
it won't take down all
the different replicas

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:02.230
of my application.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.660
On the flip side, there
are some applications,

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.547
workloads that may actually
benefit from being co-located

00:09:07.547 --> 00:09:08.380
on the same machine.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.450
For example, if you
have a web server

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:12.370
and you want to co-locate
it with the cache

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:15.070
service for better
performance and lower latency.

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:20.260
In this case, you might
want to use pod affinity.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.810
Another important aspect of
the infrastructure reliability

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:26.020
is monitoring and specifically
providing accurate signals

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:30.260
to Kubernetes about your
workload state and health.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:34.820
The first signal in this
slide is readiness probe,

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:38.170
which basically defines a
signal for when a workload is

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.300
ready to serve traffic.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:43.540
Say for example, if a workload
takes some time to start,

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:47.020
the lack of such signal may
lead to Kubernetes starting

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:50.440
to send traffic to a
not yet ready to respond

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.620
workload, which will lead
to time out and impact

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.070
its experience.

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:58.760
The second signal here
is liveness probe,

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:01.570
which provides a health
signal that Kubernetes

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:04.870
uses to determine whether
it should initiate a repair

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:09.360
and replace of a
faulty instance or not.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.280
Defining an upgrade
strategy for your cluster

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:13.620
is another important
dimension of reliability.

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:17.160
A new Kubernetes minor version
is released roughly every four

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:19.170
months from open source.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.020
And patch versions are
released more frequently.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.140
Because Kubernetes clusters
contain both control plane

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.050
and nodes, there are
two places to manage

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.750
when it comes to
keeping your clusters up

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:34.140
to date with releases, security
patches, and bug fixes.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:36.690
Luckily, GKE offers
different ways

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:39.840
to keep your cluster's
infrastructure up to date.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:42.180
Being a shared
responsibility product,

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:45.780
GKE keeps control plane
automatically updated.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:49.440
And alternatively, you can
keep your node version upgraded

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:53.250
automatically, or initiate
the upgrade manually

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:58.280
by running your own
script, or using our API.

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.770
To ensure that your
infrastructure is up to date

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:05.060
and also being kept as such in
a safe and non-destructive way,

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:08.100
we highly recommend the use
of multiple environments.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.410
So when there is a new
version or a patch,

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:16.010
it can be rolled out for
testing and qualification

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:19.280
on staging and
testing environments

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.460
before it hits the
production environment.

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:25.040
In general, we recommend
to use the same version

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:29.690
in all environments so you get
consistency and correctness

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:31.800
of your qualification process.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:34.700
However, you may want to
consider a second development

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:37.700
environment where newer
releases can be tested.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:41.870
This can be useful when a new
GKE feature or Kubernetes API

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:43.670
are being introduced,
and you want

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:48.440
to test them to get a head start
and be ready for the market

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.180
when you have a
new feature that's

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.920
relying on this capability.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:56.590
The process of upgrading
your Kubernetes clusters,

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:59.560
keeping the control plane
and nodes preferably

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:02.000
in sync when it comes
to the same version,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:05.680
and also in a supported version,
and also in a supported version

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:09.280
SKU that is mandated
by open source,

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:11.470
that process can be thorny
and very cumbersome.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:14.860
And one way to simplify it
is by using release channel.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.510
Release channel
in GKE allows you

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:18.520
to choose your upgrade path.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:20.500
It also controls
the type of upgrade

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:23.770
that will be applied
on your cluster.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:26.950
Some customers may want to use
the latest Kubernetes version,

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:28.840
because, as I mentioned
before, dependencies

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:31.720
on a new API or
capability, or maybe they

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:33.490
have a specific
business objective.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:38.080
In this case, you may want to
use the rapid channel, which

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:42.490
is closer to open source
in terms of releases.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.490
Other customers may prefer
stability over functionality.

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:47.320
And in that case,
they will choose

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:49.630
either the stable channel
or the regular channel,

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:55.500
which are the ones we recommend
for production workloads.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.650
When it comes to patching
or upgrading your cluster,

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:04.320
the nodes where the e-commerce
application are deployed,

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:05.460
we replace them.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:08.250
Those nodes are being replaced
with new provisioned machine

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:12.420
running the newer
Kubernetes GKE version when

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:14.460
an upgrade takes place.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.800
When nodes are replaced,
as you can imagine,

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.960
pods running on
them are gracefully

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:22.440
shut down so the node can
be drained and replaced.

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:24.240
On the GKE side,
node upgrade used

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.250
to follow the
workflow of delete,

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:29.370
then create, meaning
that when we upgrade,

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:33.390
GKE used to drain a node,
delete it, and then replace it

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:34.410
with a new one.

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:36.060
However, to increase
the availability

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.370
and also to reduce
disruption, node upgrade

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.160
is now happening through
surge upgrade, where

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.960
we change actually the order.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.570
We first create a new
node for the upgrade,

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:48.150
and only when that node
is ready and available,

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:50.700
we gracefully
drain the old node.

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:54.960
Not only that surge reduces
disruption to existing nodes,

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.410
it also allows you to control
the number of nodes that

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:00.270
can be upgraded concurrently.

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:02.560
The default setting
is one node at a time.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:03.870
So keep that in mind.

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:07.950
If you are using large
clusters with many nodes,

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:11.080
you probably want to
change that number

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:14.160
so more nodes can be
concurrently upgraded

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:16.350
at the same time.

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:19.200
It is clear that sustaining
a competitive e-commerce

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:23.010
infrastructure requires a
continuous upgrade practice.

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:26.550
However as we all know,
sometimes business requirements

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:29.730
may change, especially when
disruption simply cannot be

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.140
tolerated.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.480
For example, think
about peak seasons,

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:37.860
like Black Friday, Cyber Monday,
New Year, or major company

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.680
events, trade fairs,
or something like that.

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.440
We recommend you to
use an exclusion window

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:44.735
for this event.

00:14:44.735 --> 00:14:46.110
With an exclusion
window, you can

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.240
specify a range of
dates in which you

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:51.480
want to refrain completely
from any type of maintenance.

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:53.790
You can also set up
maintenance windows

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:56.880
to specify when you prefer
maintenance to happen.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:00.180
This can help you to improve
predictability of the upgrade,

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:03.690
and also allow to control
the time of the upgrades

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:06.930
on different clusters.

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:09.090
Exclusion windows and
maintenance windows

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:13.380
can greatly help you control
the disruption and reduce risk.

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:16.530
But we've seen with
many retail customers

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:18.285
that they are
actually experiencing

00:15:18.285 --> 00:15:20.160
different requirements
during different times

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:21.450
of their fiscal year.

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:23.490
For example, an
e-commerce infrastructure

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:26.280
is most likely to follow
some regulation or compliance

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:28.020
like PCI, for example.

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.220
And this compliance
has specific guidelines

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:32.910
when it comes to keeping
your software up to date

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:35.280
and how frequent and
fast a new security

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:36.930
patch should be consumed.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:40.410
In this case, I might want
to allow all type of upgrades

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.560
and patches on both
control planes and nodes

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:49.230
throughout a low season month,
while during a high peak

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:51.870
season, like Black
Friday, Cyber Monday,

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.880
I want to refrain completely
from any type of maintenance

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.940
so my workloads will
not be affected.

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:00.790
And then also in
some other cases,

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:03.960
I might be OK with just
patching my clusters, both nodes

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:06.870
and control planes,
but would want

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:10.500
to refrain from upgrading
to the next minor version.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:12.420
We believe that this
level of flexibility

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:15.510
is actually needed for
e-commerce infrastructure

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:18.420
so they can control
maintenance events,

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:20.190
and also the risk
level associated

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:24.750
with the change in accordance
to their business needs.

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:28.440
This way you can delegate all
patching and upgrades to GKE,

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:32.490
while being able to instruct
it on what type of upgrades

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:37.670
you want to allow, the scope,
and when to initiate a process.

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:41.950
So now that we know how upgrades
and patches get rolled out,

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:45.340
I think it's important to
also stay informed and know

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:49.120
about changes coming
to your clusters.

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:50.920
One place to learn
about GKE releases

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:53.750
is, of course, the GKE
Release Notes page,

00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:56.890
which includes links
to the release schedule

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:58.690
and the version support policy.

00:16:58.690 --> 00:17:02.320
But we also recommend the use
of GKE Notification, which

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:04.240
includes notification
for upgrades

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:06.069
and upgrade available events.

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:09.579
GKE Notification, which
is based on Pub/Sub,

00:17:09.579 --> 00:17:13.540
you can listen to upgrade
available events, which

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:15.460
when they fire,
indicate that there

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:19.960
is a newer version or patch
available for your cluster.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:23.380
In turn, you can use that
event and trigger a workflow

00:17:23.380 --> 00:17:25.000
to apply these
patches, for example,

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:26.359
on your testing cluster.

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:30.790
So the infrastructure version
can be vetted and qualified

00:17:30.790 --> 00:17:36.590
before it gets released to
your production environment.

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:40.000
Finally, a robust
e-commerce infrastructure

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.460
must also provide
some flexibility

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:45.100
so you can apply some
changes on the fly

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:46.870
in a non-disruptive way.

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:49.600
We've identified
several common places

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:51.610
where changes
involve disruption,

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:53.680
because when you
apply these changes,

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:57.385
it actually requires us to
recreate the machine or node

00:17:57.385 --> 00:17:58.540
pool.

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:02.230
For example, you may need to
change network tags in order

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:05.200
to change firewall rules,
or routes of your commerce

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:06.310
application.

00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:09.100
You can now do so, you
can change network tags

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:13.990
for existing running VMs,
without recreating the VMs,

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:16.540
and basically applying
this change on the fly.

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:19.570
The same thing goes
for node things, which

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:22.840
you can use to steer away
pods from specific nodes,

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.240
or labels, which are
used to schedule pods

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.730
on particular nodes.

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:32.830
All these three options are now
modifiable without requiring

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:36.550
recreation of the machine.

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:39.480
So we've learned
from the ESG report

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:43.740
that there are specific areas an
e-commerce infrastructure must

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.680
cover to maintain
competitiveness.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.840
High availability,
where redundancy

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:51.570
is important to avoid
single point of failure.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:54.360
That applies to both
control plane and nodes.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:58.845
Scalability, use multiple
replicas, the use of PDB

00:18:58.845 --> 00:19:03.090
to avoid disruption,
affinity and anti/affinity

00:19:03.090 --> 00:19:04.920
to better instruct
the Kubernetes

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:09.900
scheduler on how to optimize the
deployment of your workloads.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:13.230
Reliability and
availability, remember

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:16.680
that control planes are
fully managed on GKE,

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:18.930
but can go through maintenance.

00:19:18.930 --> 00:19:22.890
Make sure that you don't
create unnecessary dependencies

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:26.370
on the control plane
API in your application

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:29.640
to avoid disruption
to your application

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:32.220
when control plane
is being upgraded.

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:34.620
And then, finally,
for flexibility, there

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:37.290
are some changes that
can be applied on the fly

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:41.550
and do not impose any disruption
for existing workloads.

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:49.460
To close up, I want to share
a quote from Loblaw, a GKE

00:19:49.460 --> 00:19:52.780
e-commerce customer, who
achieved great success using

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:57.010
both GCP and GKE, despite
going through the unexpected

00:19:57.010 --> 00:19:58.990
challenges of the pandemic.

00:19:58.990 --> 00:20:01.780
Loblaw migrated and
modernized its e-commerce

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:05.900
with GCP and GKE, and also
leveraged the Black Friday,

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:09.340
Cyber Monday white glove service
for additional peace of mind

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:12.190
during this critical
business period.

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:14.500
This resulted in better
performance at scale,

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:17.080
faster application
development and deployment,

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:20.150
high availability, and
elimination of Black Friday,

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:22.230
Cyber Monday outages.

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:25.080
And while keeping the
infrastructure up to date,

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:28.110
Loblaw managed to leverage
the different infrastructure

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:31.560
capabilities to roll out their
COVID-19 vaccines delivery

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:37.440
system in nine weeks
instead of 18 months.

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:38.700
There's much more to learn.

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:40.740
You can download
the full ESG report

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:44.290
from the first link on this
page and find more documentation

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:49.160
best practices on the GKE
website on the second link.

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:51.470
Thank you so much for
watching this session.

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:55.740
I hope you enjoy the
rest of your Google Next.

